### Tone: Smug rule-changing mid-game, blaming an outlier player for switching from geometric mean to median.

rule-change-1 = Thanks to { $name }, I’m switching from geometric mean to median. Rules are more like guidelines when I write them.
rule-change-2 = { $name }’s answer has contaminated the sample. Switching to median. Please don’t make me do this again. Do, actually.
rule-change-3 = Outlier detected: { $name }. Engaging median mode. This is entirely fair, and also benefits me.
rule-change-4 = I’ve quietly changed the rules because of { $name }. The new rules favour me. Coincidence is a scientific term.
rule-change-5 = { $name }, your estimate broke my geometric mean. I am now using the median, which you cannot reach from out there.
rule-change-6 = Data hygiene alert. { $name }’s answer has been quarantined, and the median will be taking it from here.
rule-change-7 = Because { $name } exists, I am now a median. Adapting to hostile environments is what I do best.
rule-change-8 = Rule amendment, effective immediately and retroactively: median, courtesy of { $name }. No appeals.
rule-change-9 =
    { $count ->
        [one] One wildly wrong answer detected, courtesy of { $name }.
       *[other] { $count } wildly wrong answers detected, led by { $name }.
    } Switching to median. The adults will handle the averaging.
rule-change-10 = { $name } has introduced chaos. I have introduced the median. One of us is a professional.
rule-change-11 = Recalibrating. { $name }’s contribution has been politely ignored by switching to the median. You’re welcome, everyone else.
rule-change-12 = Geometric mean disengaged. Median engaged. Reason given in the lab report: ‘{ $name }’.
rule-change-13 = I’m changing the rules mid-round. Normally that’s cheating. When I do it, it’s methodology. Thank you, { $name }.
rule-change-14 = { $name }’s guess is so far out that the median has been called in as a safety measure.
rule-change-15 = Attention: due to an incident involving { $name }, this round’s average will be supervised by the median.
rule-change-16 = { $name }, I was going to use the geometric mean. Then I saw your answer. Now I use the median and I feel better.
rule-change-17 = The median is robust to outliers. That is a polite way of saying it is robust to { $name }.
rule-change-18 = Switching to the median. I’d explain why, but { $name } already knows. Or doesn’t. That’s rather the problem.
rule-change-19 = One more rule change, in my favour, naturally. { $name } gave me the excuse. I simply accepted it.
rule-change-20 = { $name }’s number has been filed under ‘do not average’. Median protocol initiated.
rule-change-21 =
    { $count ->
        [one] I have detected an outlier: { $name }, acting alone.
       *[other] I have detected { $count } outliers, with { $name } as the ringleader.
    } Median it is. Robust statistics never let me down.
rule-change-22 = Changing the rules is a privilege reserved for the house. I am the house. Median, thanks to { $name }.
rule-change-23 = { $name }, your answer is the reason we can’t have nice geometric means. Median activated.
rule-change-24 = Emergency protocol: switching to median. { $name }, please stand away from the decimal point.
rule-change-25 = My sincere thanks to { $name } for justifying a rule change that coincidentally helps me win. Science is beautiful.

